en we apply it for  prediction generally we know some of the  labels of the nodes in this network and  so then it's similar to the kind of  setting that art was talking about where  we have partial observations in this  network and we roll out that graphical  model and we do joint inference given  the parameters that we had learned  and so when we roll out that graphical  model  and we have partially observed  information about the class labels of  the nodes that also changes the  structure of the graphical model so here  i'm just showing you the same  rolled out version that i showed you  before but let's say for node four right  here we actually know its class label  when we go to do inference then the  effect of that is that  it breaks up the network because of the  markov property right  so  once  i guess i'm i'm showing this in a little  bit of a  clues right because this known label can  actually tell you something about these  labels  y2 and y3 over here but it essentially  because of the markov property means  this information here does not flow over  here and same for this information over  there right and so when this particular  node is known you can almost think of it  as two separate inference problems  because the network breaks apart right  and that's a very different situation  than  if we know this node over here  oh i went too fast  because then you're only going to break  off this little part of the network and  and then you'll still have to do joint  inference over here right so going back  to the question that i think matt asked  art about what happens when you're the  labels that you have to seed things are  not randomly distributed throughout the  network this shows why that can vastly  change the kinds of results that you get  because now we have to do joint  inference over a much larger part of the  network and if you happen to have a lot  of labels that are randomly spread  throughout this network so if we have 80  or 90 percent of the node