nt  to preserve the connectivity network  structure and the other issue is what  can we do  in here that takes into account that  we're doing collective inference  so  so very quickly  hoda in her dissertation worked on a  relational resampling method so that we  could get different versions of the same  graph to use for training and what we  ended up doing is sampling subgraphs  with replacement which sort of goes back  to this discussion we have about  clusters as being the sort of  informative units of the graph we have a  relatively ad hoc way of connecting  those cl those sub graphs back up to  produce a large graph so there's a lot  more work that could be done in this  area but then we also developed a way to  do  interleaving of the collective inference  processes to aggregate instead of  waiting to the end of the collective  inference process we aggregate the  predictions during the collective  inference process and so what that would  look like is that you have three graphs  here where the edges between them  the nodes are all the same but the edges  between them might be different because  of the different graphs and what you're  going to do is you're going to be making  a prediction  with one model in one graph but you have  three different graphs that you've  sampled with replacement and what you'll  do is make your individual predictions  you might not have been able to catch  that but this node right here is mostly  blue and this is mostly blue but in this  graph they think it's mostly yellow  right and so what we'll do is we'll  average those predictions before we go  to the next round of inference and this  guy will become mostly blue because the  other ones were blue right and so this  interleaving of the aggregation while  we're doing collective inference  helps to prevent errors being propagated  through the network because of some  strange graph structure that you're  seeing just because of variants in the  data okay  and so here's some results 